\f0\b\fs36 \cf2 Regional Considerations in Rehabilitation\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs24 \cf2 While central sensitization and general load management are foundational, applied sports rehabilitation also requires an intimate understanding of specific regional kinematics and functional anatomy. The shoulder complex, the lumbar spine, and the knee present unique biomechanical challenges that dictate specific exercise interventions.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa160\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs28 \cf2 The Scapular Complex and Shoulder Pain\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs24 \cf2 The scapula serves as the crucial biomechanical bridge between the axial skeleton and the glenohumeral joint. During overhead movements\'97such as a tennis serve or a barbell press\'97it is responsible for transferring kinetic energy from the lower extremities and core into the arm. Abnormalities in scapular position and kinematics, termed scapular dyskinesis, are strongly associated with chronic upper quadrant complaints, including subacromial pain syndrome, rotator cuff pathology, and cervical neck pain.\
Cools et al. provide a science-based clinical reasoning algorithm for rehabilitating scapular dyskinesis, differentiating the underlying mechanisms into two primary categories: soft-tissue inflexibility and muscle performance deficits.\

