\f0\b\fs36 \cf2 Conclusion\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs24 \cf2 The modern rehabilitation of athletic injuries requires transcending the outdated biomedical model. By integrating pain neuroscience education, athletes can reconceptualize their pain, reducing the threat value of symptoms and mitigating the devastating effects of the fear-avoidance cycle. Coaches must operationalize this knowledge through precise load management, utilizing the Acute:Chronic Workload Ratio to build robust tissue capacity while avoiding dangerous spikes in training volume. Whether dealing with tendinopathies requiring heavy slow resistance, scapular dyskinesis necessitating targeted motor control, or chronic low back pain responding to generalized movement therapy, the solution invariably lies in controlled, progressive loading. Finally, by transitioning rehabilitation from controlled closed-skill environments to chaotic open-skill scenarios, and remaining vigilant for medical red flags, coaches can safely and effectively navigate athletes through the continuum from injury back to peak performance.\
\
