\f0\b\fs36 \cf2 Parameter 3: Training Frequency Distribution\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs24 \cf2 The algorithmic utility of training frequency (e.g., 2x versus 3x per week) is widely misunderstood. While the posterior probability for strength gains increasing with frequency is 100%, the probability for hypertrophy is less than 100%, indicating that frequency's independent effect on muscle size is negligible once weekly volume is equated.\
The AI utilizes frequency strictly as a fatigue-management divisor. Because intra-session neural fatigue degrades electromyographic (EMG) amplitude as a workout progresses, forcing a high volume of sets (e.g., 12+ sets for a single muscle) into one session limits motor unit recruitment in the latter half of the workout. The system automatically partitions volume into two or three weekly sessions whenever the daily prescription exceeds 8-10 sets per muscle group. This maintains the mechanical tension of each individual set without falsely attributing hypertrophic superiority to the frequency variable itself.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa160\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs36 \cf2 Parameter 4: Load Spectrums and Repetition Ranges (5-30)\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs24 \cf2 The AI architecture decouples the parameters for strength acquisition and tissue hypertrophy. A pooled analysis of standardized mean differences demonstrates that while training with high loads (>60% 1RM) elicits superior dynamic 1RM strength gains, hypertrophic adaptations are identical irrespective of load magnitude, provided the volume load is equated and sets approach momentary failure.\
This data unlocks vast programming flexibility. The system can prescribe repetition ranges anywhere from 5 to 30 repetitions without compromising structural adaptations.\
